#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147376 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "bd1d2ef2-9472-5e57-b071-fbbaf80023da" :question/math-academy-id "e-6731" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0.$ Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = t$\n\n- $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = 0$" :question/worked-solution "The differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nis forced if $f(t)$ is not identical to zero, and damped if $b>0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify each equation.\n\n- Equation I represents forced harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=t$ is not always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0.$\n\n- Equation II represents forced harmonic motion without damping. The forcing function $f(t)=e^t$ is not always zero, but there is no damping term since $b=0.$\n\n- Equation III represents free (non-forced) harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=0$ is always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "11a44451-5b9f-5892-b321-925b2a393551" :question/math-academy-id "e-6732" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 6\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=3t^2\\,\\textrm{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t) .\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3 y'' =-9y' -6 y+3t^2.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 3y'+2y = t^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147378 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "aa77f4ef-67b6-5041-9a3b-1a36ffd5582d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6733" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y' + 5 y = 50t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y =Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}+25t+5$\n\n- $y = R e ^ {- t} \\cos \\left( 2t + \\phi \\right) +10t - 4$\n\n- $y =e^{-t}\\left( A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)+10 t-4$\n\nNote that $A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = y_c(t)+y_p(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution $y_c(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation $y''+2y' + 5 y = 0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function $y_c(t)$ is can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t).$ Note that the external force $f(t)=50t$ is a linear function. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha = 10$ and $\\beta = -4.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t)= 10 t - 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+10t-4, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+10t-4, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+10t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147379 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/id #uuid "20b53dff-43b9-5256-ab7d-67aff56db408" :question/math-academy-id "e-6734" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y =2e^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "This is an initial value problem. Since the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and $y'(0)=0.$ So, we want to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y =2e^{-t}, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c(t)$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 1,\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t).$ Note that the external force $f(t) = 2e^{-t}$ is an exponential function, and the constant factor in the exponent $(-1)$ is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha  e^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined. \n\nSubstituting $y_p$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha=1.$ Therefore, the particular solution $y_p(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) =e^{-t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t} + e^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. Substituting $y(0)=0$ gives $A=-1,$ and differentiating $y$ and substituting $y'(0)=0$ gives $B=1.$\n\nSo, our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-\\cos t + \\sin{t} +e^{-t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\  & =(1+{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d36d31-3513-5ca5-8100-812b7912783a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333040} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8444c0-a8f0-5745-ab2b-8746db8e5526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333392} {:answer/id #uuid "3f6207f8-7e2a-5666-bf53-96422217ed44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333588} {:answer/id #uuid "59eaf665-38a3-5635-ab7a-0276be907a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333810} {:answer/id #uuid "d06b3063-bb77-5eb7-b9ae-7f5d926acc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a8444c0-a8f0-5745-ab2b-8746db8e5526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333392} :answer-field/id #uuid "04a7d702-adda-5168-93a6-866f181c2ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31bc7182-8785-59ea-9a66-360f3942567f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64554" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=10{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+{e}^{-2t}$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nOnly I and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2741a969-ac92-593b-bd63-41c5cb39e9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+15y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333347} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd7e563-29bb-5b64-86a7-d736a73a9e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333593} {:answer/id #uuid "827cf43e-5b0f-5826-8445-b446c50ef603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+15y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334154} {:answer/id #uuid "995757ad-d1dd-55f1-b889-facb9c7b6a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+15y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334344} {:answer/id #uuid "ede896aa-b9cf-541d-b0a8-5094e93b446c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fd7e563-29bb-5b64-86a7-d736a73a9e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333593} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c0e3bd2-74e0-56e9-9f19-381bf23a2455" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9df2fe93-bc75-52a9-b88c-221b0f80d0b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-64555" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-15y+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0ae7afc-795b-5349-bc3e-1189d0f43bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 3y^{′} + 7y = 4\\cos (t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146775} {:answer/id #uuid "08ff4b54-707c-5e24-88bb-d7a096a92dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 14y = 8\\cos (t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146776} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab15400-e1ec-56cd-b573-c1670b7da7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 14y = 4\\cos (t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146777} {:answer/id #uuid "46862148-3dec-503c-bf71-9e55da016b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 7y = 2\\cos (t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146778} {:answer/id #uuid "56fcda2c-0410-5a99-a2cd-b358f0bac9a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 3y^{′} + 7y = 8\\cos (t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146779}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3df2eceb-b4b9-5cc8-a437-11f8d8f29b3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146774}] :question/id #uuid "98c54443-aa10-5205-9d6e-97ff4dfc9f89" :question/math-academy-id "q-64556" :question/problem "A particle of mass $2kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 14kg/s^{2}$. The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c = 6kg/s$, and an external force given by $F(t) = 8\\cos (t)N$.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbff926b-9464-55e4-bea0-0a95d63332b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1) = (\\sin 2 + 3)m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146799} {:answer/id #uuid "0045288f-c559-5b17-a27e-67f2a3b6f87d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1) = (\\cos 1 - 2)m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146800} {:answer/id #uuid "053fa0a8-bee7-5495-a910-f3f146ca22b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1) = (2\\cos 3 + \\sin 3 - 1)m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146801} {:answer/id #uuid "4400bf40-eb70-5eb3-84be-0a0e332cc631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1) = (\\cos 2 + 1)m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146802} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba546f7-d7b6-5922-be6f-07995204d489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1) = (\\cos 1 + \\sin 1 + 3)m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146803}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7e4eb606-49f5-5bde-8a87-5a33db5fb64e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146798} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d69d6c7b-6c0f-5833-b358-024e1b2373a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡1+3)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392865} {:answer/id #uuid "141a0a74-e302-523c-9f8c-bad2145b337b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392866} {:answer/id #uuid "bc71a800-3396-5d41-b999-dc1cc70c1cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-2)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392867} {:answer/id #uuid "d183e142-084c-5c40-a40a-3e43c9bd291f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2+3)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392868} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2b2af7-c2a5-5791-bb97-7ee22ccd83a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3-1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "141a0a74-e302-523c-9f8c-bad2145b337b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392866} :answer-field/id #uuid "2118b5c2-d188-5b07-bc58-c6425a8b5a57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d1bc0c7-99f9-59d0-919b-05c0a19b6daa" :question/math-academy-id "q-64572" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=8{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $1$ second." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {t}^{2}+\\beta t+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{t}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=1,\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after one second, we substitute into the equation $t=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(1)+2(1)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea038c9b-fd10-5f9d-b8c9-dc05c276d7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 16y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146823} {:answer/id #uuid "ef15dd6a-b331-53c1-b8fc-273620fa29e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} - 16y = 4e^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146824} {:answer/id #uuid "f26441c9-ac54-5ce2-bb51-e68eb4186364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 25y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146825} {:answer/id #uuid "00761822-e83f-58a4-806d-fc36c4dfd8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 5y^{′} + 4y = e^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146826} {:answer/id #uuid "7257a4aa-56b4-566e-a420-ffb1e87a474a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 10y^{′} - 9y = 6t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146827}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8e8e210b-6a79-5d2c-9abd-92d31ac86dff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146822}] :question/id #uuid "74f88e96-11ac-578d-832b-92585f234521" :question/math-academy-id "q-64594" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion with damping?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b95bd28-2983-56cd-9bcc-bbf3be6b8e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333242} {:answer/id #uuid "2112540c-83b9-5e31-a8e8-64651d567624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-16y=3{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333284} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd5a079-828e-5a58-a7bf-3c10cbf7ffae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+10y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333403} {:answer/id #uuid "a84fb22f-0363-5cc1-8c3c-7163c3e57f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+25y=4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334463} {:answer/id #uuid "ac246792-bb97-5619-97d3-f01f9ff448d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-25y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a84fb22f-0363-5cc1-8c3c-7163c3e57f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+25y=4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334463} :answer-field/id #uuid "48a4a815-26d6-58f2-92a7-843941c349ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1617e3b1-0c82-5e4f-9ee5-ab5a7b9879bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64596" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion without damping?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=4{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56cc10ba-89e7-5521-bc2d-573108d183c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(2-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333774} {:answer/id #uuid "5a143907-ceba-56f1-a9b4-520726bc291e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(1-\\pi )\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333814} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b9dee-11e2-5aac-aeb4-657f75ba9cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(3+\\pi )\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334392} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3e4293-24eb-5088-b2f2-9fc987990176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334787} {:answer/id #uuid "ec25efeb-5809-5f21-97f2-7ae5aecda325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce3e4293-24eb-5088-b2f2-9fc987990176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334787} :answer-field/id #uuid "e57d283d-d783-5d66-b8cb-ced36172bad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b5683d4-f207-5c86-9f78-566919581fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-64643" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.$ The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ because this is already included in the complimentary function. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is  \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=t(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.  \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =0,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+(B+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=0,\\,B=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=(1+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =(1+2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8f52905-5d8b-5d17-8ebd-7067e851cdfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146979} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0a5530-cef9-5e37-8866-e82eee8b62ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146980} {:answer/id #uuid "c97020b2-beeb-5600-97ed-991001d0ffaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146981} {:answer/id #uuid "258b25cf-fe72-53f7-afd8-ea4ef9f234e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146982} {:answer/id #uuid "501a8c4c-591b-547c-aa37-4fe0ea0fe4b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146983}] :answer-field/id #uuid "67d3b07f-4b66-57da-824f-5775d382ccb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146978}] :question/id #uuid "9aa670fe-3757-54df-85dc-a7b3def25d33" :question/math-academy-id "q-79461" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 25y = 17\\sin t$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = e^{-3t}(A\\cos 4t + B\\sin 4t) - \\frac{1}{6}\\cos t + \\frac{2}{3}\\sin t$\n2. $y = Ae^{-3t} + Be^{4t} + 17\\cos t + 17\\sin t$\n3. $y = Re^{-3t}\\sin (4t + ϕ) - \\frac{1}{6}\\cos t + \\frac{2}{3}\\sin t$\n\nNote that $A$, $B$, $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02dd969f-3835-50e2-8bfc-d425532d61e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345375} {:answer/id #uuid "90284f75-7093-5853-a1bf-dfd348956afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346606} {:answer/id #uuid "a6006a37-315c-535b-84c5-4aae73a3316f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346800} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e5dd56-c7d7-5b38-9d36-b6ef7d0b527a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347087} {:answer/id #uuid "f4150680-d532-528d-904e-713e4f2b8907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7e5dd56-c7d7-5b38-9d36-b6ef7d0b527a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347087} :answer-field/id #uuid "43f9a281-44ca-5645-8aea-5eda107eeda2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41288342-236a-5f59-a0ab-b7381746dd7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79575" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=40\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$\n- $y=R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n- $y=R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022d4245-6ff2-570a-a5d1-209de982ed36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354674} {:answer/id #uuid "65948148-93b5-522f-adb5-08d74855dc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-2y={t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355568} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdce21d-40be-5b14-b940-fe2419ec67d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y=1-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356308} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a71987-a9fe-56fe-bc54-607962e29496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+11y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356554} {:answer/id #uuid "db738211-d361-5a0d-860e-1ca27db32400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7a71987-a9fe-56fe-bc54-607962e29496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+11y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356554} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dcb0b4b-3c35-568e-929d-5f743e4ea885" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1618a66f-ae17-56b8-a5fd-31bbc1677763" :question/math-academy-id "q-94229" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion without damping?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+11y=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cc4dbf-2cf7-50aa-bbe7-509d47070250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354734} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1b6885-759b-57c4-b9a8-b9f42753444a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+13y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356301} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4b69ce-aeae-5464-a9cd-fb68e74147e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356337} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf21c36-0252-5de0-b48c-c5a157513b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356457} {:answer/id #uuid "e777cb41-fcbb-502e-bf49-fd5ae4af094d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf21c36-0252-5de0-b48c-c5a157513b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356457} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1310cdd-b690-5715-8274-04ab3b8ca773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f86e92b-34ea-56e9-a272-eb80bc579c17" :question/math-academy-id "q-94230" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion without damping?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60dd2038-a894-57a6-af28-585a167caf9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355523} {:answer/id #uuid "70ba6f37-7912-534e-af4e-354edb9d8929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+18y=6{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355660} {:answer/id #uuid "754922b1-7b42-56db-904e-15ceab6c74fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{3}{2}y=3{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355695} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4365e4-97d4-5eac-b202-4e562e6c8b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6y=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355903} {:answer/id #uuid "c22c70d9-9504-53f5-ab68-407f3b367e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3y={e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60dd2038-a894-57a6-af28-585a167caf9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355523} :answer-field/id #uuid "54732efb-666c-5bf8-9c4f-c02a10e39c3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a91fb75-0041-5c82-8578-4977beec3c1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94232" :question/problem "A particle of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ An external force $F(t)=9{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n6{y}^{″}=-18y+9{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1225434f-ee3b-5bcb-a463-c2289b6fbb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354829} {:answer/id #uuid "347d73f4-d32c-5f40-b3b8-3bc248cebbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+4y=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355130} {:answer/id #uuid "3aed3bad-5173-5e43-be0b-6618315c8154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2y=4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355195} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd1390d-5c20-5eb7-ba12-10587b4a1690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{2}y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356723} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd76e0b-97e7-54a6-b48d-c22a7cc5257f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+5y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1225434f-ee3b-5bcb-a463-c2289b6fbb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354829} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcad8b9e-d911-573b-9e0d-630986594286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a59a904-03e2-5485-b6c2-876a20fc36bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-94233" :question/problem "A particle of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=16\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n8{y}^{″}=-16{y}^{′}-10y+4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25213c17-424a-52a3-9371-0722697b6bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404688} {:answer/id #uuid "fe19165f-f1ce-59b5-81e9-0ecaae91fcfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404689} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9a43fb-7d21-5c00-92a4-f2ea33cb0a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404690} {:answer/id #uuid "928f1857-0069-5902-a97e-67cf179eed13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404691} {:answer/id #uuid "cd83c873-17b3-53db-8554-5059d348276f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd83c873-17b3-53db-8554-5059d348276f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404692} :answer-field/id #uuid "039eefc7-4800-59f4-a9af-2eb142d7c4e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f3b61bf-2d9c-5a4f-b827-01f0e0d4bec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94304" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in centimeters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=49\\,⟹\\,\\omega =7.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,\\beta $ are constants to be determined.\n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2},\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{2})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
